---
/**
 * website/src/pages/pricing.astro — the pricing page (the subscriptions plan, Task S7.2; the
 * owner's rulings D403, D430 and T22).
 *
 * **What the page is.** Three tiers — Community, Team, Enterprise — and the features each one
 * includes, which is the "Tiers and features" table of `docs/subscriptions/spec.md` and nothing
 * else. The plan's Change line for this task is exactly that page, and it is the page T22 asks
 * for: self-serve buyers need to see the tiers before they buy.
 *
 * **Why the tiers and the rows are written HERE rather than in a component or in a loaded
 * file.** `internal/cli/docs_subscriptions_test.go` (S0.1) is the gate that keeps a price,
 * a discount, a fee rate and the Enterprise minimum out of this public repository, and it reads
 * a page's copy *and its frontmatter* while it deliberately does not read the components a page
 * composes (its own comment says so, and says this page is where the Team price would land). A
 * page whose data sat behind a component would be a page whose figure the scan cannot see, so
 * the data lives in the frontmatter of the file the scan reads. `website/test/pricing.mjs` reads
 * the built page, and between the two a figure has nowhere to hide.
 *
 * **The price is published, not committed** (D430, T22). The subscription documents own every
 * figure, the Team price is inserted at the owner's publication (S7.4), and what stands in its
 * place here is `TEAM_PRICE` below — a sentence, not a number, because there is no number this
 * repository may carry. The page's test pins that sentence and refuses a currency amount or a
 * percentage anywhere on the page, so the publication is the commit that changes both, and it
 * is the one edit that makes this page a price list.
 *
 * **The rows are the plan's, and the test is what holds them to it.** The twelve feature rows
 * come from `docs/subscriptions/spec.md`, and `website/test/pricing.mjs` reads that table and
 * holds the built page's rows to it — the same names, the same order and the same three answers
 * per row. Reading the table at build time instead would put the site's own words at the mercy
 * of a markdown edit (a table the plan reflows is a page that stops building), and it would take
 * the row names out of the file S0.1's scan reads; a test keeps the two in step and fails loudly
 * when a row moves, which is the failure the owner wants to see.
 *
 * **A cell is a mark or nothing, never a word.** The three columns are the same three tiers the
 * features page prints as its plan columns, and they answer the same question, so they answer it
 * the same way: a check where the tier includes the feature and an empty cell where it does not
 * (`features.astro`'s rule, and the same reason — the page chooses no word for the state that is
 * not a mark). The check is not `aria-hidden`: nothing beside it says what it is, so a reader who
 * cannot see the glyph hears its own name, and the column's heading says which tier it answers.
 *
 * **The one call to action is a link the owner supplied.** Enterprise is "contact us" (T22), and
 * the site's contact route is the booking link `website/data/landing.yaml` holds — the same value
 * the campaign pages' Book a demo buttons use, read here through the same loader, so the link has
 * one home and a link that moves moves everywhere. A plain `<a href>`: nothing is loaded from
 * Calendly's host by this page, so no consent gate stands in front of it. No e-mail address is
 * published anywhere on the site (the owner's ruling of 2026-10-19, held by `test/privacy.mjs`).
 *
 * **The three links under the table are the questions a reader of a price list has next**: what
 * each feature does (`/features/`), what the software costs them beyond the subscription, since
 * it runs in their own cloud project and pays its own model bill (`/caps-and-costs/`), and the
 * agreement the paid tiers are sold under (`/legal/subscription-terms/`).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadLanding } from '../lib/landing.mjs'

/** The route's word, and the page's `<h1>`. */
const TITLE = 'Pricing'

/**
 * The page's lead, which is also its `<meta name="description">`: the three tiers in one
 * sentence, so a search result carries what the page is. Nothing stands between the `h1` and
 * this paragraph — D188 reads a page's lead as the text between its `h1` and its first `h2`
 * (`budget.mjs`), and the ceiling is 40 words.
 */
const LEAD =
  'Community is free forever, Team is self-serve and priced per seat, Enterprise is annual and invoiced. This page says what each one includes.'

/**
 * The Team price, and the whole of what this page may say about it while it is unpublished: the
 * figure is published at the owner's publication (S7.4) and never enters this repository (D430,
 * T22), so the price line carries the sentence below instead of a number. It is a constant with
 * one use rather than a literal in the markup because the publication replaces this value and
 * nothing else — see the file's header for why it sits in this page at all.
 */
const TEAM_PRICE = 'Price published at launch'

/**
 * The three tiers, in the order `spec.md` states them, each with the spec's own words for how it
 * is bought: "Three tiers: Community (free forever), Team (self-serve, per seat) and Enterprise
 * (annual, invoiced)". The spec's fourth fact about a tier — what it costs — is the one that is
 * not here: it is either published (Team, at S7.4) or a contract's (Enterprise, whose contact
 * link is the way in).
 */
const TIERS = [
  { key: 'community', name: 'Community', sold: 'Free forever' },
  { key: 'team', name: 'Team', sold: 'Self-serve, per seat' },
  { key: 'enterprise', name: 'Enterprise', sold: 'Annual, invoiced' },
] as const

/**
 * The plan's "Tiers and features" table, row for row: the feature's name as the spec writes it,
 * and which of the three tiers includes it. A row a reader would ask about — the seat rule, the
 * licence, the free programs — is not this page's to add: the table is the plan's, and
 * `website/test/pricing.mjs` holds this list to it.
 */
const FEATURES = [
  { name: 'CLI, cloud jobs, recipes, harnesses', community: true, team: true, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Spend caps, kill switch, credentials security service', community: true, team: true, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Rigor modes, including Hotfix', community: true, team: true, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Fugaro Watch dashboard', community: true, team: true, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Console and mobile app, single user', community: true, team: true, enterprise: true },
  {
    name: 'Console and mobile app, multi-user with viewer/operator/admin roles',
    community: false,
    team: true,
    enterprise: true,
  },
  { name: 'Per-project coordinator agent', community: false, team: true, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Shared central orchestrators', community: false, team: true, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Hosted MCP server with policy-narrowing tokens', community: false, team: true, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Workspaces: shared identity, credentials, workspace-wide cap', community: false, team: false, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Audit and spend-history exports', community: false, team: false, enterprise: true },
  { name: 'Contract terms, security review, invoicing', community: false, team: false, enterprise: true },
] as const

/** The Enterprise tier's way in: the owner's booking link, from `website/data/landing.yaml`. */
const BOOKING_URL = loadLanding().calendlyURL
---

<Base title={TITLE} description={LEAD}>
  <article class="pricing" data-pricing-page>
    {/* The first screen: the page's word, then the lead. The band is a `<div>` and not a
        `<header>`, because it is the page's opening and not a landmark — the site's landmarks
        are the layout's (`website/test/a11y.mjs`). */}
    <div class="pricing-hero">
      <h1 class="pricing-title display-xl">
        <span class="gradient-text">{TITLE}</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="pricing-lead">{LEAD}</p>
    </div>

    {/* The three tiers: one card each, in the spec's order, with the spec's own words for how
        the tier is bought. The Team card carries the price line — the placeholder, until the
        owner's publication puts the figure there — and the Enterprise card carries the one
        call to action on the page. */}
    <section class="pricing-section" aria-labelledby="pricing-tiers">
      <h2 class="pricing-heading display-md" id="pricing-tiers">The three tiers</h2>

      <ul class="pricing-tiers">
        {
          TIERS.map((tier) => (
            <li class="card pricing-tier" data-tier={tier.key}>
              <h3 class="pricing-tier-name">{tier.name}</h3>
              <p class="pricing-tier-sold">{tier.sold}</p>
              {tier.key === 'team' && <p class="pricing-tier-price">{TEAM_PRICE}</p>}
              {tier.key === 'enterprise' && (
                <p class="pricing-tier-contact">
                  <a href={BOOKING_URL}>Contact us</a>
                </p>
              )}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    {/* The table: one row per feature of the plan's table, and a column per tier. The feature's
        name is the row's header cell, so the three marks beside it are read against a row a
        screen reader has just named. */}
    <section class="pricing-section" aria-labelledby="pricing-table">
      <h2 class="pricing-heading display-md" id="pricing-table">What each tier includes</h2>

      <table class="pricing-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Feature</th>
            {TIERS.map((tier) => <th scope="col" class="pricing-tier-head">{tier.name}</th>)}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            FEATURES.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" class="pricing-feature">{row.name}</th>
                {TIERS.map((tier) => (
                  <td class="pricing-included">{row[tier.key] ? <span class="pricing-check">✓</span> : null}</td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    {/* The three questions a reader of a price list has next, each a link to the page that
        answers it: what the features do, what the software costs them beyond the subscription,
        and what the paid tiers are sold under. The seat rule is stated rather than linked — a
        seat is what the per-seat price is charged for, and it is the one term a reader of this
        page cannot be left to guess (D433's pricing rule). */}
    <div class="pricing-notes">
      <p class="pricing-note">
        A seat is a person who launches jobs, by any means — the console, the app, the command line or an agent.
      </p>
      <p class="pricing-note">
        What each feature does, and what is still to come, is on the <a href="/features/">features page</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="pricing-note">
        Fugaro runs in your own cloud project, so the model and infrastructure bills are yours — the{' '}
        <a href="/caps-and-costs/">caps and costs</a> page says how they are capped.
      </p>
      <p class="pricing-note">
        The <a href="/legal/subscription-terms/">Subscription Terms</a> are the agreement the paid tiers are sold under.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): every colour below is the palette in force, so the contrast the site's
     accessibility check computes is the contrast a reader gets. The page adds no colour of its
     own, no `z-index`, and moves nothing. */

  .pricing-hero {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding-top: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  /* The headline's own measure, so the one word does not run the width of a wide window. */
  .pricing-title {
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  /* The lead: one step up the type scale, the quieter tone, and the measure — the shape the
     site's other pages set their lead in. */
  .pricing-lead {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .pricing-section {
    padding-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .pricing-heading {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
  }

  /* The three cards: one column on a phone, three on a wide viewport, each card the same box
     (`.card`) so the tiers read as one row of equal offers rather than three lists. */
  .pricing-tiers {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .pricing-tiers {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }

  /* A card: its own column, so the price line and the call to action sit under the name
     whatever length the words above them are. */
  .pricing-tier {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  /* How the tier is bought — the spec's own words — is the card's one line of type at the
     reading size: it is what a reader compares across the three cards. */
  .pricing-tier-sold {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  /* The price line: the quietest line of the card and the one that is a sentence while the
     figure is unpublished, so it is set apart from how the tier is bought rather than beside
     it. It is also what gives the Team card the room the Enterprise card's link needs, so the
     three cards' lines start and end together. */
  .pricing-tier-price {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .pricing-tier-contact {
    margin: var(--space-2xs) 0 0;
  }

  /* The table: the site's own defaults (`base.css`), with the room a table needs above it when
     it follows a heading rather than a sentence. */
  .pricing-table {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  /* A fixed grid on a wide viewport, so a mark sits under its own tier's heading at every
     width: the Feature column is what is left of the three tier columns, which are wide enough
     for the longest tier name. A phone keeps the browser's own sizing, since `base.css` narrows
     a table to the viewport there and fixed columns would leave the feature name no room. */
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .pricing-table {
      table-layout: fixed;
      width: 100%;
    }

    .pricing-table thead th:first-child {
      width: calc(100% - 24rem);
    }

    .pricing-tier-head {
      width: 8rem;
    }
  }

  .pricing-feature {
    font-weight: var(--weight-normal);
  }

  /* A tier's name over its column, and the marks under it: centred, so the three columns read
     as three answers a reader scans down. */
  .pricing-tier-head,
  .pricing-included {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* The mark in a column of its own, the width of a glyph, so the checks line up under their
     headings rather than drifting with the row's text. It is the cell's whole content and it is
     not `aria-hidden`: beside it there is no word saying yes, so a reader who cannot see it
     hears the glyph's own name. */
  .pricing-check {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 1em;
  }

  /* The four lines under the table: the page's last words, each a question a buyer asks next.
     They are set in the quieter tone because they are notes rather than the offer — except the
     links in them, which are the body's tone because they are what a reader acts on. */
  .pricing-notes {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .pricing-note {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
